-- Passage rg7-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8cafda76-e587-5302-bd0a-4cd85cba334e', 'rg7-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$A Hive Is a City$q$, $q$Scientists often compare a honeybee hive to a busy city. The comparison is not perfect, but it helps explain how thousands of insects can work together without a mayor giving orders.

Like a city, a hive has many jobs that must be done every day. Young worker bees stay inside and act as nurses, feeding the developing larvae. Older workers become builders, shaping wax into six-sided cells. The oldest workers become foragers, flying out to collect nectar and pollen.

The wax comb is the city's housing and its warehouse at the same time. Some cells hold growing young bees, while others store honey for winter. A healthy comb is packed so tightly that not a single space is wasted.

Communication keeps the hive running. A forager that finds a rich patch of flowers returns and performs a "waggle dance." The angle and length of the dance tell other bees where the flowers are. In a sense, the dance is the hive's map and its news report rolled into one.

The queen is often misunderstood. Her title suggests that she rules the hive, but she does not give commands. Her main job is laying eggs, up to about two thousand a day in summer. If the hive is a city, the queen is less like a ruler and more like the source of every new citizen.

When the weather turns cold, the bees cluster together in a tight ball and shiver to make heat. The whole colony becomes one living furnace, keeping the queen warm until spring.$q$, 'draft')
