-- Passage rg7-figurative-language-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d65270b0-2b8f-5ced-95de-c4c1fd0ad78c', 'rg7-figurative-language-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Grandfather's Workshop$q$, $q$Every Saturday, Mateo Villanueva visited his grandfather's workshop behind the house. The old garage smelled of sawdust and machine oil. Tools hung on the walls in neat rows, each one waiting for its turn.

Grandpa Rafael had repaired clocks for forty years. His hands were spotted and wrinkled, but they moved as steadily as a surgeon's. Mateo liked to watch him lift tiny gears with tweezers and set them in place.

This Saturday, Grandpa handed Mateo a dusty mantel clock. "This one has been asleep for twenty years," he said. "Let's see if we can wake it up."

Mateo opened the back panel. Inside was a jungle of brass wheels and springs. He felt his confidence sink. "I don't even know where to start," he admitted.

"A clock is like a team," Grandpa said. "Every gear depends on the gear next to it. Find the one that stopped doing its job, and you will find the problem."

They worked side by side for two hours. Mateo cleaned each wheel and checked each tooth. Finally, he spotted a bent pin that kept one gear from turning. Grandpa straightened it with pliers, and Mateo wound the spring.

For a moment, nothing happened. Then the clock cleared its throat with a soft whir and began to tick. Mateo grinned so wide his cheeks hurt.

"You see?" Grandpa said, tapping the glass. "Patience is the best tool in this whole shop."$q$, 'draft')
