   $q$The narrator says Elijah's argument "was a fireworks show: loud and bright, but over in a flash, leaving nothing behind but smoke." What does this metaphor suggest?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It was calm and quiet, so the class had trouble hearing it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-detail"},{"text":"It included a video with fireworks to grab the class's attention.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It was exciting to watch but had little real support behind it.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It was the longest and most detailed argument in the debate.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It was exciting to watch but had little real support behind it.$q$, $q$Fireworks are flashy but quickly gone, so the metaphor means Elijah's speech was showy but left "nothing behind," unlike Soo-ah's survey and study. Being over "in a flash" is the opposite of a long, detailed argument.$q$, 'draft', 'a13b98dc-7e03-5ab6-8a07-82bb3be47390')
