-- Passage rg7-figurative-language-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('756a1566-b585-55bb-ad40-7a1ea1811c87', 'rg7-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Rescuing Millbrook Creek$q$, $q$For years, Millbrook Creek was the town's forgotten backyard. Plastic bags clung to the branches along its banks, and old tires sat half buried in the mud. Most people drove past without a second glance.

That changed when a seventh-grade science class at Lakeview Middle School began testing the water. The students, led by their teacher, Mr. Nakamura, found that the creek was choking on trash and runoff. They presented their results at a town meeting, and the room went silent.

The cleanup that followed became a true odyssey. Like the long journey of the ancient Greek hero Odysseus, it was full of delays and surprises, and it lasted far longer than anyone expected. A storm washed new trash downstream the week after the first cleanup. A permit for removing the tires took four months to arrive.

Still, volunteers kept coming. On the biggest cleanup day, more than two hundred people flocked to the creek with gloves and buckets. Retirees worked beside toddlers and their parents. A neighborhood bakery donated muffins, which disappeared faster than snow in July.

Two years later, the creek is a different place. Small fish dart through the shallows again, and a family of herons has returned to the marsh. The students still test the water every month, and they post their results on a sign near the footbridge.

Mr. Nakamura says the project taught his students that a town's natural spaces are like a shared library book. Everyone gets to enjoy them, and everyone is responsible for returning them in good condition.$q$, 'draft')
