   $q$Mr. Nakamura says a town's natural spaces "are like a shared library book." What relationship does this analogy show?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Natural spaces can be borrowed once and then kept by whoever wants them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-analogy"},{"text":"People should read about nature instead of visiting real outdoor places.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Everyone may use them, and everyone must take care of them for others.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Only the people who pay a fee are allowed to enjoy the town's creek.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Everyone may use them, and everyone must take care of them for others.$q$, $q$He explains that "Everyone gets to enjoy them, and everyone is responsible for returning them in good condition," just like a library book. Keeping something you borrowed goes against the "returning" part of the analogy.$q$, 'draft', '756a1566-b585-55bb-ad40-7a1ea1811c87')
